## Remove participant rejoin code

### Status
Implemented.

### GitHub Issue
#73

### Summary
Guest participants currently must enter a rejoin code (a 6-character code shown on the
event home page) to re-select an existing guest display name from the sign-in dropdown.
The client has decided this validation layer is unnecessary — guests should be able to
pick any existing guest participant name from the dropdown without proving they're the
original owner.

### Motivation
The rejoin code adds friction for guests and the client no longer wants that layer of
validation. Removing it simplifies the sign-in flow for the common case (a group of guests
sharing one event link) at the cost of guests no longer being distinguishable from one
another by identity — an accepted tradeoff.

### Proposed Changes
1. Remove rejoin-code enforcement from `EventsController.SignIn.cs` (`RequiresRejoinCode`,
   the code-validation branch in `TryUpdateExistingParticipantAsync`, and the
   `RejoinCode`/`ShowRejoinCodeInput` population in `BuildSignInViewModelAsync`).
2. Remove `RejoinCode`/`ShowRejoinCodeInput` from `EventSignInViewModel` and the
   rejoin-code markup/JS from `SignIn.cshtml`.
3. Remove `RejoinCode` from `EventHomeViewModel` and the display line from `Home.cshtml`;
   remove it from `EventsController.Home.cs`.
4. Remove the rejoin-code backfill in `EventsController.AccessCookie.cs`
   (`GetCurrentParticipantAsync`) and `UniqueCodeGenerator.GenerateUniqueParticipantRejoinCodeAsync`.
5. Update `CODING_CONVENTIONS.md` to remove the rejoin-code enforcement rule.
6. Add and apply an EF Core migration dropping `Participant.RejoinCode`.

### Acceptance Criteria
- The sign-in form no longer requires or displays a rejoin code input for guest
  participants selecting an existing display name.
- The event home page no longer displays a rejoin code.
- No new rejoin codes are generated anywhere (sign-in flow and the access-cookie fallback
  both currently generate one).
- `Participant.RejoinCode` column is dropped from the database via an EF Core migration.
- The account-ownership guard (`HasConflictingAccountOwner`), which blocks a signed-in
  user from selecting a participant display name already linked to a *different*
  signed-in account, is unchanged — this is not the rejoin code and must not be weakened.
- The browser access cookie (`WhenWorksWeb.EventAccess.{CODE}`) that recognizes a
  returning guest's own browser is unchanged.

### Out of Scope
- Any new guest identity/verification mechanism to replace the rejoin code — the client
  has explicitly decided guests will not have that layer of validation going forward.
- Updating existing tests that reference rejoin codes (`EventSignInViewModelTests`,
  `EventsControllerSignInTests`, `EventsControllerHomeTests`, `ParticipantBuilder`,
  `SqliteDbContextFixture`) — per CLAUDE.md testing policy, tests are only modified on
  explicit separate request.
